course: dictionary
lesson: 8402
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. Rule is one of them, so you need the context to know which meaning the writer wants. «Pool rules are about slipping, pushing, drains and never swimming without a grown-up watching.» Here a rule tells you what must or should be done.
explain: Rule can also be a verb, a doing word. «The queen ruled for years» means she was in charge of a country. In maths, a rule tells you how to get the next number, as in «add 3». Read the words around the word, then try your meaning in the sentence to check it fits.
explain: Some phrases use rule in special ways. «As a rule» means usually, so «as a rule, we walk to school» means most days. «Rule out» means decide that something is not possible. Look for the small words around rule, because they change its meaning.
fact: A rule tells what must or should be done, like school rules or the rules of a game.
fact: In maths, a rule tells how to get the next number, or how to change an input into an output.
fact: To rule means to be in charge of a country, and to rule a line means to draw it with a ruler.
fact: «As a rule» means usually, and «rule out» means decide that something is not possible.
fact: «Rules are made to be broken» is a saying, not good advice; a fair rule is the same for everyone.
exercise: «Pool rules are about slipping, pushing and drains.» What does rules mean here? | Numbers that go up by three | The time a king was in charge | *Things that must or should be done | Lines drawn with a ruler
exercise: «The queen ruled for years.» What does ruled mean? | *Was in charge of a country | Won every game | Drew a straight line | Decided something was not possible
exercise: «As a rule, we walk to school.» Find and copy the two words that mean usually. | school | walk | we | *As a
test: In the number pattern 2, 5, 8, 11, the rule is «add 3». What does rule mean here? | *How to get the next number | To draw a line with a ruler | To be in charge of a land | A school rule about being on time
test: «The storm ruled out a picnic.» What does ruled out mean? | *Decided a picnic was not possible | Ate the picnic food | Made a new picnic rule | Drew a line for the picnic
exam: Mia says rule and ruler are the same word. What is the best answer? | Yes, and both mean usually | Yes, they mean exactly the same thing | *No, a ruler is a tool for drawing lines, while rule has several meanings | No, rule is only ever a verb
